# exam-solver MCP server

A minimal MCP server (Streamable HTTP transport) exposing exactly one tool,
`solve_challenge`, which reads the exam challenge from the live HTTP
request's headers on every call — not from the JSON-RPC body.

## Endpoint

Once deployed, the MCP endpoint is:

```
https://<your-project>.vercel.app/mcp
```

(`/mcp` — that's the official MCP SDK's default streamable-HTTP path.
Vercel serves this whole app as one Function and lets its internal
Starlette router match sub-paths, so `/mcp` resolves correctly with no
extra routing config.) **Give the grader the full URL including `/mcp`.**

## Why the official `mcp` Python SDK instead of hand-rolled JSON-RPC

Streamable HTTP has real protocol details (Accept-header negotiation,
optional SSE vs. plain-JSON responses, protocol version negotiation,
optional session IDs) that a real MCP client will exercise. Hand-rolling
it risks subtle incompatibilities with whatever client the grader uses.
The official SDK (`mcp`, the same one used to build MCP servers/clients
generally) is built and tested against the real protocol, so this uses
it directly:

```python
mcp = FastMCP("exam-solver", stateless_http=True, json_response=True)
```

- `stateless_http=True` — every request is handled independently, no
  server-side session store. This also happens to be required for a
  serverless deploy target like Vercel, since nothing guarantees two
  calls land on the same warm process.
- `json_response=True` — respond with a single `application/json` body
  instead of opening an SSE stream, since `solve_challenge` only ever
  returns one message.

## How the per-call header read works

The tool function takes a `Context` parameter:

```python
@mcp.tool()
def solve_challenge(ctx: Context) -> str:
    request = ctx.request_context.request  # the raw Starlette Request for *this* call
    challenge = request.headers.get("x-exam-challenge") or ""
    return hashlib.sha256(f"{challenge}:{REGISTERED_EMAIL}".encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
```

The SDK threads the actual per-request `Request` object through
`ctx.request_context.request` for each individual JSON-RPC message — so
even though `tools/list` and all five `tools/call`s belong to one logical
MCP session, each is its own HTTP POST, and this handler reads that
specific request's headers, not a cached copy from `initialize`.

`Context` parameters are automatically excluded from the generated JSON
schema, so `solve_challenge`'s `inputSchema` comes out as
`{"properties": {}, "type": "object"}` — no required properties, as
specified. Verified locally (see below).

`X-Exam-Signature` isn't checked: verification is explicitly optional/
unscored, and there's no way to check it anyway without the exam-only
HMAC secret, which isn't given to the server.

## About that 421 error

If you deployed an earlier version of this file, you may have hit
`HTTP 421`. That turned out to have nothing to do with Vercel routing
or `vercel.json` — it's the MCP SDK's own DNS-rebinding protection.
`FastMCP` auto-enables a Host-header allowlist whenever `host` is left
at its default `"127.0.0.1"`, permitting only `localhost` / `127.0.0.1`
/ `::1`. That's meant to stop a malicious webpage from tricking a
browser into hitting a local dev server under a trusted-looking name —
it doesn't apply to a public HTTPS API, and left on, the SDK rejects
every real request (whose Host header is your actual `*.vercel.app`
domain) with `421 Invalid Host header`, before your tool code ever
runs. Confirmed locally by spoofing the `Host` header against a plain
build (`421`) vs. this one (`200`).

Fixed by explicitly passing:

```python
transport_security=TransportSecuritySettings(enable_dns_rebinding_protection=False)
```

to `FastMCP(...)`. This file already has that fix.

## Local testing

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt httpx --break-system-packages
uvicorn api.index:app --port 8812 &
MCP_TEST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8812/mcp python3 test_mcp_client.py
```

`test_mcp_client.py` is a raw HTTP client (not the high-level MCP client
SDK) that mimics the grader exactly: `initialize` →
`notifications/initialized` → `tools/list` → five `tools/call`s, each
with a **freshly generated** 32-hex-char `X-Exam-Challenge` header, and
checks the returned text against an independently computed
`sha256(challenge:email)[:16]` for each one. All 5 passed in testing,
plus the schema check.

## Deploy to Vercel

Vercel's current Python runtime auto-detects this with **zero configuration** —
no `vercel.json` needed. It looks for a top-level `app` (ASGI/WSGI) in a
recognized entrypoint file (`app.py`, `index.py`, `server.py`, `main.py`,
`wsgi.py`, or `asgi.py`, at the project root or under `src/`, `app/`, or
`api/`) and serves your whole app as a single Vercel Function — every
request goes to that app and its own router matches the path. `api/index.py`
already satisfies this, so this folder deploys as-is.

(If you're deploying this project after an earlier attempt that included a
`vercel.json` with a `builds`/`routes` block — that's the old, now-legacy
config format and can cause routing errors like a 421. Just delete it;
none is required here.)

**Via CLI:**

```bash
npm i -g vercel
cd exam-solver-mcp
vercel --prod
```

**Via GitHub (your usual flow):** push this folder to a repo, then
Vercel dashboard → New Project → Import → Deploy. No env vars needed.

Once deployed, sanity-check with curl (note the two Accept types are
both required by the streamable-HTTP spec):

```bash
curl -i -X POST https://<your-project>.vercel.app/mcp \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Accept: application/json, text/event-stream" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"curl","version":"0"}}}'
```

You should get back a 200 with a JSON-RPC result containing
`serverInfo` / `capabilities`.

**Keep it deployed and warm through the grading window** — the grading
note says a passing run earlier doesn't count if the server is down when
the live check actually runs.
